WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.809 --> 00:00:16.240
I am Jess Holz, and I normally make artwork
with the scanning electron microscope to create

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.599
these sort of alien landscapes.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:25.609
This time, I actually imaged the moon and
the sun with my dad's telescope.

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:33.440
I was just sort of struck by how peering down
the eyepiece of a telescope feels a lot like

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.000
peering down the eyepiece of a microscope.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.170
Just the way you have to sort of hold your
head and eyes at a certain angle and the way

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:43.670
the image sort of evades you.

00:00:43.670 --> 00:00:48.390
I also looked at the sun so this is just through
my cell phone through a filter.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:52.159
This is through the telescope now, kind of
like, in the palm of your hand.

00:00:52.159 --> 00:00:55.559
And then the next slide here is the final
image.

00:00:55.559 --> 00:01:00.220
And you can see a little dark speck, a sunspot
on it.

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:05.810
And I was sort of amazed at the fact that
the sunspot could be possibly the size of

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:06.880
the earth.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:14.770
Now in microscopy you see a lot of junk like
maybe little dark specks or a lot of debris,

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:19.000
but usually these things are like 100 nanometers
and, you know, very inconsequential.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.240
But on the sun this thing is like the size
of the Earth.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.709
That just kind of blew my mind.

